tigation to deferred: the marginal contrast has now been discovered and independently estimated, but its threshold exceedance remains imprecise. The related conditional feature_063 claims provide stronger evidence for meaningful negative residual contrasts in specific strata, so the productive next step is not another marginal contrast but a better-powered residual contrast.\n\nThe strongest supported findings in this dataset are the core measured tags. feature_010=1 is associated with a large positive composite dependency score: main claim H35 was +0.829 (+0.767 to +0.891), and many conditional estimates remain similarly large. feature_002=1 is associated with a large positive score and has independent validation: H53 discovery +0.810, validation +0.803 (+0.706 to +0.900); H55 within feature_010=0, discovery +0.780, validation +0.808 (+0.727 to +0.889). feature_037=1 is associated with a large negative score under the negative-direction convention: main claim H32 was signed +0.804, corresponding to a raw decrease of about 0.804; the decisive conditional residual claim H74 within feature_010=0 and feature_002=0 was discovery +0.814 and validation +0.845 (+0.770 to +0.921). These findings are robust dataset-specific associations and argue against one-way redundancy among feature_010, feature_002, and feature_037, but they remain observational associations rather than causal effects.\n\nThe feature_063 evidence now supports at least conditional negative associations, even if the marginal threshold claim remains unresolved. Because H80 and related claims are registered with negative direction, positive signed estimates mean raw negative contrasts. Accepted conditional claims include H81 within feature_010=0 and feature_002=0: +0.255 (+0.165 to +0.345); H82 within feature_037=0: +0.247 (+0.151 to +0.342); H84 in the triple-absent stratum feature_010=0, feature_002=0, feature_037=0: +0.252 (+0.169 to +0.335); and H86 within feature_002=1: +0.365 (+0.180 to +0.550). These